# Cyclone (Khan, Anand, Brown and Baker, arXiv:2511.15910), as the team's transport deck writes it
# for QCCD Studio: one closed ring of traps, the data standing still and the ancillas rotating in
# lockstep.  Not the rule set `cyclone`, which is the paper's artifact as Reproduce replays it.
#
# Y1-Y4 are the deck's S1-S3 and HW1 where no R rule says the same thing.  The deck's S4 is R4b,
# its S5 R27 and R12, its HW3 R3 and R5.  Its S6 assigns stabilizers on the fly under R26: Goal 3
# keeps the circuit's order instead (the project owner's decision, 2026-11-06), so this set uses
# R25.  Its S8 and S9 are design choices.  Its S10 assumes cooling at no cost; the team's feedback
# (Ke, 2026-11-07, Q2) gives Cyclone the cooling rule and time of the other two, so its ions heat
# and R7 is judged as theirs is.  R34 is the feedback's F2: a laser step addresses one chain
# position, even with each trap's laser switched by itself.  R13 is left out: HW1 allows 30 ions a
# trap, which is Y4.

ruleset "cyclone-deck";
title "Cyclone's rules, as the deck writes them";
about "One closed ring of traps with L-shaped junctions at its corners. Data qubits never move. Every ancilla moves in lockstep, one trap per step, one way round. A trap does one operation at a time, a step's gates come before its rotation, and a laser step reaches one chain position in every trap it plays in. Each qubit's operations keep the circuit's order.";

use R0, R1, R2, R3, R4, R4b, R5, R6, R6b, R7, R8, R11, R12, R18, R19, R20, R21, R24, R25, R27, R34;

param serial_kinds = {"gate2", "split", "merge"};
use Y1, Y2, Y3, Y4;
